very case, however, he will express heteropatriarchal masculinity, and control over experience—even if he struggles against poverty, or the impact of drugs and gambling.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 9) “and when it presents Indians as participating in the same heteropatriarchy as settlers, it ignores the colonial imposition of notions of masculinity, control, and gender identity (see Deer, 2015;Miranda,2010; TallBear, 2019).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 10) “Across all of these contexts, the settler ignorance that is sedimented by country music authorizes violence against Indigenous women, as well as the continued theft of land, resources, ancestors, and identities.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 10) “we both find much to love and ponder about Outlaw Country, as well as other country subgenres. But engagements with country music should aim to cultivate more resistant epistemologies, which interrogate the sociopolitical imaginaries that structure country music, and the vicious sedimentation that fortifies settler ignorance.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) “we explore two techniques for building more resistant epistemologies, using the scaffolding of Outlaw Country: epistemic chronostratigraphy and trickster hermeneutics.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) “‘Epistemic chronostratigraphy’ designates a parallel set of practices, which are employed to analyze the layers of settler sociopolitical imaginaries and the resulting patterns of fortified ignorance.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) “These practices involve recognizing, naming, dating, and explaining the creation of the layers in these ecologies, and identifying what is taken for granted and promulgated by the knowledge sources we inherit from our ancestors.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) “In Whyte’s (2018) discussion of vicious sedimentation, epis- temic chronostratigraphy requires investigating how Michiganders come to know the land as they do; and it requires highlighting causes of cultural amnesia, such as the absence of Anishinaabe history from Michigan public schools” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) “Finally, in the context of Outlaw Country’s thickly sedimented layers of settler epistemic ecologies, epistemic chronostratigraphy takes the form of excavating the lineage of this music, and analyzing the sediments that this music deposits by invoking and preserving specific kinds of sociopolitical imaginaries” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) “1. Recognize fortified settler ignorance as the top layer of a sedimented reality; 2. Explain how taken-for-granted aspects of the settler colonial imaginary come to be known; and, 3. Acknowledge that since knowledge and ignorance are sedimented realities, it is possiblethough very difficult—to carve pathways through this sedimented reality that are less destructive and more liberatory.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 12) “The Indigenous characters on this album are real people, facing real challenges, and trying to cope with an ongoing history of genocide.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 12) “This song portrays an interracial relationship without exoticizing tropes, and it criticizes conservative racist norms (La Chapelle, 2007, p. 205).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 12) “Many have been communal undertakings, including name changes, community dialogues around which songs to cover and which songs to drop from juke boxes, and other attempts at shedding layers of manifest manners. But there is a lot of work left to do, and only time will tell if any of these interventions will stick.9” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 12) “But the caricatured identities (or manifested absences) that are sedimented in the genre often become obstacles. To navigate ‘simulations’ of Indians and manifested absences, Indigenous people often develop a skill set that Vizenor (1999) terms ‘trickster hermeneutics’. Put much too simply, surviving in a settler colonial world often requires cultivating skills for interpreting and strategically navigating the Indian identities imposed by settler sociopolitical imaginaries. This might involve strategically playing the ‘good Indian’ when the white man is looking, a strategy that has been cultivated since settlers first arrived on Turtle Island.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 13) “Consider the robust engagements with country music in Indigenous communities, which require using the epistemic resources that are entrenched in colonial constructions of Indigenous identities. As we noted above, depictions of Indigenous communities are either non-existent or opposed to cowboys in a great deal of country music, and one might assume country music isn’t popular among Indigenous communities. But country music, Wranglers, cowboy hats, and boots are massively popular in Indigenous communities, and there are many Indigenous country artists.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 13) “When Stateline (2004) sings “reservation road, take me home” they invoke and simultaneously subvert generations of colonial sediment.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 13) “The trickster hermeneutics enacted by Indigenous country artists thus transform sedimented Indian identities of country ecologies into sovereign assertions of Indigenous existence and resistance.10” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 13) “Their song Black Myself, is a straight-up country banger, which explores perspectives that are commonly excluded from country songs, including the perspective of an enslaved person, a person struggling against racial segregation and Jim Crow laws, a person struggling to succeed in a world organized by racial capitalism and structural racism, and a person pursuing forms of co-flourishing centered on Black power and Black communities.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 14) “For those who only perceive the top layer of a viciously sedimented reality, country music might seem to provide depictions of real people, struggling with real challenges, and trying to navigate a real world” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “It might also offer playful explorations of working class, Southern, and rural identities that are excluded from other popular media” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “But in either case, the process of vicious sedimen- tation and the internalization of settler colonial imaginaries can make country music seem like three chords and the truth, more or less” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “This is the legacy of colonialism and white supremacy; it is the core of manifest manners; and it makes it difficult to convince those who benefit from the legacy of settler colonialism to carry out the epistemic labor that is required to cultivate chronostratigraphic skills.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “As we have noted throughout this paper, Indigenous peo- ple are often depicted as mere simulations within country music and other contexts” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “Still, we hope that our discussion of epistemic chronostratigraphy in country music provides hermeneutic resources for making this experience of epistemic fatigue intelligible; and we hope to have highlighted some of the reasons why people who must routinely dedicate epistemic and practical resources to navigating spaces shaped by vicious sedimentation are not the ones who need to do the relevant epistemic labor.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “Against this backdrop, it is all the more impressive that there are people who have engaged in trickster hermeneutics, revealing pathways toward co-flourishing within marginalized com- munities.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “Country music is often about kinfolk; and the country music community has often been a fairly tight family; so, outlaws routinely ask listeners to stop and think it over, and to put themselves in their position (Williams, 1979).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “But there is enough communal structure in country music to build a richer understanding of community, and a richer understanding of how our perspectives are bound to our history. Like other family traditions, country music helps people to become who they are (Dyck, 2021);” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “possibilities for co-flourishing. There is an important truth that country music can teach those who benefit from the entrench- ment of settler colonialism:” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) “Outlaws come at things in their own way; and outlaw epistemologists are committed to learning how to read patterns of vicious sedimentation that authorize settler colonial imaginaries, and learning about the complex history of the machinations of settler colo- nialism” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 16) “But whether you saddle up to take your horse down that old town road (Lil Nas X, 2019) or hope the reservation road will take you home (Stateline, 2004), you should always have tools for constructing more resistant and resilient epistemologies, and building richer structures for co-flourishing, in your holster” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 16)
