\textbackslash section \textbackslash par “In discussing these two issues, I highlight the importance of community in country music.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 1) \textbackslash par “As a result, simple art is good for building community. Simple melodies are easy to learn, so they can spread faster.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 8) \textbackslash par “We have a common conception of country music as involving folkloric authenticity; according to this conception, it seems natural or inevitable that real country involves pure self-expression and comes from certain races.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 9) \textbackslash par “First, country authenticity is fabricated through images and marketing; the music industry created an impression that country music came from a longstanding musical rural tradition.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 9) \textbackslash par “There is no essential ‘hard core’ sound; it merely contrasts against mainstream “soft shell” pop trends, which change in each decade. But hard core country music is read as more authentic.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 10) \textbackslash par “Early country music created a market for new songs that sounded old.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 10) \textbackslash par “It is the responsibility of the country artist (insofar as they want to be recognized as authentic)” (Malone, 2022, p. 14) \textbackslash par “In this way, we should think about authenticity discourse as the linguistic field of a larger aesthetic game (with its own set of moves, rules, and officers)” (Malone, 2022, p. 14) \textbackslash par “Tradition is also acknowledged by progressives when it comes to race. Stoney Edwards’ classic “Blackbird (Hold Your Head High)”” (Malone, 2022, p. 21) \textbackslash par “Meanwhile, otherwise traditionalist artist Tyler Childers’ “Long Violent History” connects protests over racist police brutality in the present to Appalachian union coal miner’s history of resistance (such as in the Battle of Matewan) in order to make progressive moves in the practical identity game.” (Malone, 2022, p. 21) \textbackslash par “This is not all to say that country music is, on average, progressive, but just that its” (Malone, 2022, p. 21) \textbackslash par “For these three reasons, the concept of country music authenticity has been constructed from commercial interests.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 10) \textbackslash par “Country also grew substantially from popular music in the Southern United States. This popular music was thoroughly racially integrated (Miller); it had dominant roots in genres of Black music, such as jazz and the blues.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 11) \textbackslash par “If we claim that country music is irreversibly racist, we ignore its history. Artists such as the Carolina Chocolate Drops and Rhiannon Giddens have sought to recover the tradition of Black hillbilly music.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 13) \textbackslash par “Kyle Whyte (2018) describes the process of” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 3) \textbackslash par “Vicious sedimentation occurs in numerous contexts, pervading the material and epistemic landscape. Just as terraforming introduces non-native species and contaminants into indigenous ecosystems, settler sociopolitical imaginaries are layered upon one another until they become unquestioned realities in settler society; and just as landscape architecture can—sometimesreveal the effects of vicious sedimentation, epistemic disruptions can—sometimes—make the epistemic sediment visible.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 4) \textbackslash par zotero://note/2202160/DR9X94HH/“The second issue is country’s ideal of authenticity; fans and performers think that country should be ‘real’. \textbackslash par “Country plays an important role in many indigenous communities throughout North America—after all, First Nations reservation life is often rural (Witmer 1973, Whidden 1984, Dueck 2013, Jacobsen 2017).” (Dyck, 2021, p. 14) \textbackslash par “My claim here is not that the content of country music songs always explicitly encode racist meanings, only that the genre’s context gives it strong racist associations. While the association is sometimes strong, it is just an association rather than an essential part of the genre. This should give us hope.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 14) \textbackslash par “Authenticity often becomes a flag for more general issues about where one’s personal ideals lie.” (Dyck, 2021, p. 15) \textbackslash par “Some argue that authentic country music is a matter of the artist having a biography that supports the rural and working-class identity of country protagonists” (Malone, 2022, p. 1) \textbackslash par “others argue tha” (Malone, 2022, p. 1) \textbackslash par “Country Music and the Problem of Authenticity | 2 authenticity in country music is merely a matter of the music manifesting a rural American mythology that carries a certain emotional resonance (Shusterman 1999).” (Malone, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “a matter of demonstrating and embodying the core value commitments of the genre” (Malone, 2022, p. 3) \textbackslash par \textbackslash sectionMeissner \textbackslash par “Abstract” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 1) \textbackslash par “Settler colonial imaginaries are constructed through the repeated, intergenerational layering of settler ecologies onto Indigenous ecologies; they result in fortified ignorance of the land, Indigenous peoples, and the networks of relationality and responsibility that sustain co-flourishing.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 1) \textbackslash par “vicious sedimentation.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 1) \textbackslash par “that Outlaw Country music plays important roles in sedimenting settler imaginaries” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 1) \textbackslash par “clarifying the epistemic dimensions of vicious sedimentation” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 1) \textbackslash par “then explore specific cases where Outlaw Country songs function as epistemic scaffolding for maintaining and preserving deeply sedimented settler imaginaries.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 1) \textbackslash par “we conclude by considering ways of using country music as epistemic scaffolding for constructing resistant epistemologies” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 1) \textbackslash par “INTRODUCTION” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “But what kinds of truths can people learn while listening to country music?” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “Perhaps country music preserves memories of the ‘mountain music’ that grandma and grandpa used to play (Owen, 1981).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “more aware of the struggles that loving mothers and fathers face, as they raise eight kids on a miner’s pay (Lynn, 1970).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “recognize a deeper connection to Main Street, where people love living right and being free (Burris \& Haggard, 1969).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “The genre has been continually redesigned to make it sound old, ‘traditional’, working class, or Southern (Pecknold, 2007; Peterson, 1997).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “Moreover, the fact that times and places sound ‘country’ often reflects mistaken, but nonetheless taken-for-granted assumptions.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “Loretta Lynn was a coal miner’s daughter who sang from the heart.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “Indigenous identity” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “about cleaning her teepee, taking care of six papooses, and going on the warpath after learning about what her big brave chief was hunting (Lynn, 1969).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “we should look elsewhere for the ‘truths’ that structure country music.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “Finally, by focusing on engagements with country music that support a broader understanding of the world and its possibilities, we might come to understand how country music inflects the epistemic machinations of settler colonialism.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “VICIOUS SEDIMENTATION” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “Some social epistemologists focus on questions about shared knowledge, testimony, peer disagreement, or methods of judgment aggregation. But there is a growing awareness that socio-political” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 2) \textbackslash par “considerations organize our understanding of the world, and stabilize epistemic perspectives.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 3) \textbackslash par “considerations organize our understanding of the world, and stabilize epistemic perspectives.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 3) \textbackslash par “Discussions of testimonial, hermeneutic, and contributory injustice have thus become more salient in social epistemology (Dotson, 2012, 2014; Fricker, 2007; Medina, 2012; Pohlhaus, 2012); as have discussions of white ignorance, which highlight the spread as well as the active management of misinformation and errors within communities that have been shaped by colonialism and white normativity (Mills, 2007).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 3) \textbackslash par “Kyle Whyte (2018) describes the process of vicious sedimentation, which continually imposes settler ecologies onto Indigenous ecologies, fortifying settler ignorance against Indigenous peoples.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 3) \textbackslash par “Vicious sedimentation occurs in numerous contexts, pervading the material and epistemic landscape. Just as terraforming introduces non-native species and contaminants into indigenous ecosystems, settler sociopolitical imaginaries are layered upon one another until they become unquestioned realities in settler society; and just as landscape architecture can—sometimesreveal the effects of vicious sedimentation, epistemic disruptions can—sometimes—make the epistemic sediment visible.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 4) \textbackslash par “Dian Million (2013) introduces the term ‘sociopolitical imaginaries’ to characterize the embodied and affectively rich knowledge systems that arise within epistemic communities.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 4) \textbackslash par “Manifest manners are collections of biased hermeneutic resources that are available within the settler sociopolitical imaginary. They provide resources for forming settler colonial identities; and they sustain projected caricatures of Indigeneity, which come to be shared through the practices of surveillance and domination, and the hierarchized binaries of knowers/non-knowers, which constitute the core of the settler sociopolitical imaginary.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 4) \textbackslash par “The development of a settler colonial imaginary is a complex and ongoing project, which is shaped by processes of vicious sedimentation.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 4) \textbackslash par “But from a perspective that arises atop numerous layers of sedimented ignorance, the world simply seems to have the characteristics that it does, almost as a matter of necessity.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 4) \textbackslash par “The felt aspect of this knowledge informs the unspoken, commonly held ‘affectively understood’ sociopolitical imaginary that imposes racialized, gendered, and sexualized presumptions onto Indigenous people (Million, 2013).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 4) \textbackslash par “But far more generally, settlers learn to encounter specific sounds and sights as conveying meaningful information about Indians.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 4) \textbackslash par “To be clear, people do not just passively absorb sociopolitical imaginaries. They learn which aspects of the history of colonial America to pay attention to by engaging with idealized representations of cowboys, pioneers, and Indians, which commonly appear in Hollywood Westerns, comic books, and country songs, as well as formative games of ‘cowboys and Indians’ (Trouillot, 1995, p. 21); active, repeated engagement in these culturally-scripted behavior supports the development of attentional, evaluative, and cognitive skills for navigating cultural norms regarding identity and community (Markus \& Kitayama, 2010). On the one hand, active engagement with visual and narrative imagery organizes habits of thought and action; on the other hand, the stability of material and ideological structures within a community make it easier for people who share attentional and affective biases to act in socially accepted ways (Liao \& Huebner, 2021).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 5) \textbackslash par “The import of these ideas for our purposes will become clearer in the next section, but our key claim is that the narrative imagery of Outlaw Country music scaffolds the construction of settler colonial imaginaries, and supports practices of vicious sedimentation.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 5) \textbackslash par “As settler life became increasingly distant from a cowboy past, an intense interest in cowboy songs and stories began to structure country music (Malone \& Neal, 2010, p. 138)” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 6) \textbackslash par “Okie provides a simple depiction of a clean-cut ‘common man’, who lives a traditional life, in the town of Muskogee, Oklahoma.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 6) \textbackslash par “he term ‘Okie’ was often used to refer to poor white immigrants to California, who had fled ‘their land’ in Oklahoma and nearby states during the Dust Bowl and Great Depression. Haggard’s parents had emigrated from Western Oklahoma to Bakersfield, California during this period (Price, 2018,p.107);andhetookprideinhislivedunderstanding of the working-class struggles. So, it might seem obvious that this song was intended to valorize a white settler identity, which is “anti-hippie, working-class, and unsure about the cultural changes that urbanization, Civil Rights, and integration had wrought” (La Chapelle, 2007,” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 6) \textbackslash par “Okie was sometimes interpreted satirically, sometimes interpreted as depicting pride in a marginalized identity, and sometimes interpreted as acknowledging that freedom is easily taken away.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 7) \textbackslash par “But the meaning of Okie was never really up to Haggard or these countercultural icons.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 7) \textbackslash par “No matter how we interpret Okie, it is clear that it depicts the ‘common man’ as an agent, who presents his understanding of what a traditional, white, masculine heterosexual, American identity should be.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 7) \textbackslash par “This character takes pride in small-town life, about 50 miles southeast of Tulsa; he gestures to traditions and ways he thinks things have always been in this part of the world; and he seems to claim that anyone who takes pride in a traditional, small-town lifestyle can enjoy living right and being free. By contrast, Okie never presents hippies or protestors as agents. We never see their point of view; and their characteristics are only defined in contrast to those of the cleancut ‘common man’. Put bluntly, the hippies and protesters are the background that allows the characteristics of the ‘common man’ to pop-out as an identity worth preserving; and the assumed audience for this song is someone who can identify with this ‘common man’, and experience this character’s perspective as one they already (implicitly) understand.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 7) \textbackslash par “When Okie shot to the top of the country charts, it provided an alternative perspective for the ‘Silent Majority’ to identify with.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 7) \textbackslash par “Even if Okie doesn’t tell us to dislike hippies, it depicts a practical identity that can be identified with as a source of pride or a source of community; and it provides scaffolding for constructing this identity, especially for people who don’t live in the situation it depicts (compare Dyck, 2021, p. 4)” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 8) \textbackslash par “In these respects, we contend that Okie provides epistemic scaffolding for maintaining settler colonial imaginaries and projecting racialized others through manifest manners.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 8) \textbackslash par “The Tulsa Race Massacre had occurred roughly 50 miles away, just 40 years earlier, and decades of life under Jim Crow, as well as the immense racial hostility evoked by desegregating public spaces, fostered high levels of racial tension in Muskogee throughout the 1960s and the 1970s. Okie presents a top-layer caricature of this town as its only reality.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 8) \textbackslash par (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 8) "The Ballad of Curtis Lowe" depicts an epistemic scaffolding that is racially diverse. Here, poor whites and blacks shared similar experiences. There is not much reason to think that this was not necessarily the case in Muskogee as well. \textbackslash par “By ignoring these aspects of life in Muskogee, and projecting the absence of racialized others, it obscures the sedimented reality of this land.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 8) \textbackslash par “So, it was reasonable to fear that photographs and Black Tulsans’ testimony would be unable to serve as evidence of the massacre until they were authorized by white storytellers (Mills, 2007).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 8) \textbackslash par “It might seem like we have selected an easy and dated example to make our case. But examples scattered throughout the Outlaw Country genre depict a similar sociopolitical imaginary, sometimes explicitly projecting simulations of Indian identities.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 9) \textbackslash par “but take pride in their good Christian raisin’ and their eighth-grade education (Shaver, 1973).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 9) \textbackslash par “In every case, however, he will express heteropatriarchal masculinity, and control over experience—even if he struggles against poverty, or the impact of drugs and gambling.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 9) \textbackslash par “and when it presents Indians as participating in the same heteropatriarchy as settlers, it ignores the colonial imposition of notions of masculinity, control, and gender identity (see Deer, 2015;Miranda,2010; TallBear, 2019).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 10) \textbackslash par “Across all of these contexts, the settler ignorance that is sedimented by country music authorizes violence against Indigenous women, as well as the continued theft of land, resources, ancestors, and identities.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 10) \textbackslash par “we both find much to love and ponder about Outlaw Country, as well as other country subgenres. But engagements with country music should aim to cultivate more resistant epistemologies, which interrogate the sociopolitical imaginaries that structure country music, and the vicious sedimentation that fortifies settler ignorance.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) \textbackslash par “we explore two techniques for building more resistant epistemologies, using the scaffolding of Outlaw Country: epistemic chronostratigraphy and trickster hermeneutics.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) \textbackslash par “‘Epistemic chronostratigraphy’ designates a parallel set of practices, which are employed to analyze the layers of settler sociopolitical imaginaries and the resulting patterns of fortified ignorance.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) \textbackslash par “These practices involve recognizing, naming, dating, and explaining the creation of the layers in these ecologies, and identifying what is taken for granted and promulgated by the knowledge sources we inherit from our ancestors.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) \textbackslash par “In Whyte’s (2018) discussion of vicious sedimentation, epis- temic chronostratigraphy requires investigating how Michiganders come to know the land as they do; and it requires highlighting causes of cultural amnesia, such as the absence of Anishinaabe history from Michigan public schools” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) \textbackslash par “Finally, in the context of Outlaw Country’s thickly sedimented layers of settler \textbackslash{} epistemic ecologies, epistemic chronostratigraphy takes the form of excavating the lineage of this \textbackslash{} music, and analyzing the sediments that this music deposits by invoking and preserving specific \textbackslash{} kinds of sociopolitical imaginaries” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 11) \textbackslash par “1. Recognize fortified settler ignorance as the top layer of a sedimented reality; 2. Explain how taken-for-granted aspects of the settler colonial imaginary come to be known; and, 3. Acknowledge that since knowledge and ignorance are sedimented realities, it is possiblethough very difficult—to carve pathways through this sedimented reality that are less destructive and more liberatory.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 12) \textbackslash par “The Indigenous characters on this album are real people, facing real challenges, and trying to cope with an ongoing history of genocide.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 12) \textbackslash par “This song portrays an interracial relationship without exoticizing tropes, and it criticizes conservative racist norms (La Chapelle, 2007, p. 205).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 12) \textbackslash par “Many have been communal undertakings, including name changes, community dialogues around which songs to cover and which songs to drop from juke boxes, and other attempts at shedding layers of manifest manners. But there is a lot of work left to do, and only time will tell if any of these interventions will stick.9” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 12) \textbackslash par “But the caricatured identities (or manifested absences) that are sedimented in the genre often become obstacles. To navigate ‘simulations’ of Indians and manifested absences, Indigenous people often develop a skill set that Vizenor (1999) terms ‘trickster hermeneutics’. Put much too simply, surviving in a settler colonial world often requires cultivating skills for interpreting and strategically navigating the Indian identities imposed by settler sociopolitical imaginaries. This might involve strategically playing the ‘good Indian’ when the white man is looking, a strategy that has been cultivated since settlers first arrived on Turtle Island.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 13) \textbackslash par “Consider the robust engagements with country music in Indigenous communities, which require using the epistemic resources that are entrenched in colonial constructions of Indigenous identities. As we noted above, depictions of Indigenous communities are either non-existent or opposed to cowboys in a great deal of country music, and one might assume country music isn’t popular among Indigenous communities. But country music, Wranglers, cowboy hats, and boots are massively popular in Indigenous communities, and there are many Indigenous country artists.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 13) \textbackslash par “When Stateline (2004) sings “reservation road, take me home” they invoke and simultaneously subvert generations of colonial sediment.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 13) \textbackslash par “The trickster hermeneutics enacted by Indigenous country artists thus transform sedimented Indian identities of country ecologies into sovereign assertions of Indigenous existence and resistance.10” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 13) \textbackslash par “Their song Black Myself, is a straight-up country banger, which explores perspectives that are commonly excluded from country songs, including the perspective of an enslaved person, a person struggling against racial segregation and Jim Crow laws, a person struggling to succeed in a world organized by racial capitalism and structural racism, and a person pursuing forms of co-flourishing centered on Black power and Black communities.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 14) \textbackslash par “For those who only perceive the top layer of a viciously sedimented reality, country music might \textbackslash{} seem to provide depictions of real people, struggling with real challenges, and trying to navigate a \textbackslash{} real world” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “It might also offer playful explorations of working class, Southern, and rural identities \textbackslash{} that are excluded from other popular media” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “But in either case, the process of vicious sedimen- tation and the internalization of settler colonial imaginaries can make country music seem like three chords and the truth, more or less” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “This is the legacy of colonialism and white supremacy; it is the core of manifest manners; and it makes it difficult to convince those who benefit from the legacy of settler colonialism to carry out the epistemic labor that is required to cultivate chronostratigraphic skills.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “As we have noted throughout this paper, Indigenous peo- ple are often depicted as mere simulations within country music and other contexts” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “Still, we hope that our discussion of epistemic chronostratigraphy in country music provides \textbackslash{} hermeneutic resources for making this experience of epistemic fatigue intelligible; and we hope \textbackslash{} to have highlighted some of the reasons why people who must routinely dedicate epistemic and \textbackslash{} practical resources to navigating spaces shaped by vicious sedimentation are not the ones who \textbackslash{} need to do the relevant epistemic labor.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “Against this backdrop, it is all the more impressive that there are people who have engaged \textbackslash{} in trickster hermeneutics, revealing pathways toward co-flourishing within marginalized com- \textbackslash{} munities.” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “Country music is often \textbackslash{} about kinfolk; and the country music community has often been a fairly tight family; so, outlaws \textbackslash{} routinely ask listeners to stop and think it over, and to put themselves in their position (Williams, \textbackslash{} 1979).” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “But there is enough communal structure in country music to build a richer understanding of \textbackslash{} community, and a richer understanding of how our perspectives are bound to our history. Like \textbackslash{} other family traditions, country music helps people to become who they are (Dyck, 2021);” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “possibilities for co-flourishing. \textbackslash{} There is an important truth that country music can teach those who benefit from the entrench- ment of settler colonialism:” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 15) \textbackslash par “Outlaws come at things in their own way; and outlaw epistemologists are committed to learning how to read patterns of vicious sedimentation that authorize settler colonial imaginaries, and learning about the complex history of the machinations of settler colo- nialism” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 16) \textbackslash par “But whether you saddle \textbackslash{} up to take your horse down that old town road (Lil Nas X, 2019) or hope the reservation road will \textbackslash{} take you home (Stateline, 2004), you should always have tools for constructing more resistant and \textbackslash{} resilient epistemologies, and building richer structures for co-flourishing, in your holster” (Meissner and Huebner, 2022, p. 16)
\par
With the uniformity of programming that consolidation produces, it is important to note who heads these umbrella corporations. According to the Federal Communications Commission’s 2010 quadrennial report, which covers the majority of the time period of this analysis, whites owned 80 percent of all radio stations (Federal Communications Commission 2012). Additionally, whites are cited as the largest consumer base for rap music with the total rap consumer base being described as anywhere from 60 to 80 per cent white (Bialik 2005; Kitwana 2002; Rose 2008). Thus, white interests are doubly shaping mainstream rap music; white corporate interests shape the rap industry while white consumers’ demand drives a particular brand of rap lyrics, one that is defiantly and “authentically” black (Hahn 2014; Samuels 1991).
